LEAD = {
    "headline": "YOUTUBE NOW PUTS A MODELLED GUESS AT HOW MANY PEOPLE WERE ON THE SOFA INSIDE THE PUBLIC VIEW COUNT, SO THE NUMBER ON YOUR BRAND FILM IS NO LONGER A COUNT OF PLAYS, AND IT IS THE SECOND TIME THE RULER HAS MOVED IN UNDER THREE MONTHS",
    "deck": "The view count is the one number everybody in this industry trusts without checking.  As of yesterday it contains an estimate.  If three people watch your film together on a television, that can now register as three views rather than one, worked out by a model whose method has not been published.  There is a way to back it out, and that way disappears in May 2027.",
    "stamps": [
        ("PPC LAND  16 NOV", "https://ppc.land/youtube-adds-estimated-tv-co-viewers-to-public-view-counts"),
        ("NET INFLUENCER  16 NOV", "https://www.netinfluencer.com/youtube-adds-estimated-tv-co-views-to-public-view-counts/"),
        ("DEXERTO  17 NOV", "https://www.dexerto.com/entertainment/youtube-now-shows-co-views-in-big-shake-up-to-view-counts-3428863/"),
    ],
    "body": [
        "YouTube's own words, posted <mark>16 November</mark>: \"Starting today, public view counts on video watch pages will include estimated co-views.\"  And the admission of what changed: \"Until now, one TV playback has only counted as a single public view.\"  On a phone, a tablet or a computer, one play is still one view.  On a television set, one play can now count as several, depending on how many people a model thinks were in the room.  The announcement went out as a Creator Insider Short, which had <mark>335 views</mark> about 24 minutes after it was posted, so a change to the most quoted number in online video shipped to an audience of roughly a packed lecture theatre.",
        "Now the size of it, because that decides whether you care.  YouTube's own worked example puts a co-view rate of <mark>1.10</mark> on 1,000 television playbacks, which produces <mark>1,100 views</mark>, an uplift of about <mark>10 percent</mark>.  That is one illustration rather than an average.  The range it could sit in is wider: the trade body VAB, working with the measurement firm TVision, puts <mark>45 percent</mark> of the adverts served on YouTube's connected-television screens as co-viewed against <mark>60 percent</mark> for premium streaming, and a Nielsen finding from 2022 put <mark>26 percent</mark> of YouTube television-screen viewing as having more than one adult present against <mark>22 percent</mark> for linear television.  Against a base of <mark>more than a billion hours a day</mark> of YouTube watched on television sets, and YouTube sitting at <mark>15.8 percent</mark> of all United States television time against Netflix on 7.7, this is not a rounding error on a small slice of the audience.",
        "The part that should actually stop you is that this is the second redefinition in under three months.  On <mark>24 August</mark> YouTube changed a public view to count from the first frame of playback, including autoplay in a feed.  On <mark>16 November</mark> it added modelled co-viewers.  So if you put this year's brand video next to last year's and compare the view counts, you are comparing across two changes to what the word means, in the same direction, both of which make the newer number bigger.  Any year-on-year chart in a deck you are about to present is measuring the ruler as well as the video.",
        "What has not been published is the method.  Net Influencer's account describes an estimation model built by Google research scientists that judges when more than one person is in the room using demographic patterns, content metadata and viewing times.  YouTube is clear about one thing, that it \"never uses cameras, microphones, or device sensors to measure co-views.\"  Beyond that there are no error margins, no data sources, no update frequency and no accuracy claim anywhere.  The co-view rate also varies by video, by audience and by region, and can move as co-views are added, so two videos on the same channel can carry different multipliers and you will not be told what either one is unless you go looking.",
        "Here is the useful part, and it is the reason this is a decision rather than a complaint.  You can back the estimate out.  In Studio Insights, under Advanced Mode and then Device Type, there is a <mark>co-view rate</mark>.  Divide total views by that rate and you have the count without co-views, which is the number that is comparable to everything you measured before yesterday.  Separately, <mark>engaged views</mark>, which is YouTube's stricter count of plays that pass a minimum watch threshold, <mark>do not include co-views</mark>, and neither do creator payouts, so the money and the stricter metric are both unaffected.  The catch is the deadline: the Views Co-viewed figure stays in Advanced Mode only <mark>until May 2027</mark>, after which the inflated public number is the one that is easy to get and the clean one is the one you have to work for.",
        "One consequence for anyone who commissions creator video, which nobody announced.  A creator can now show you a larger view count for the same film, legitimately, without anything about the film changing, and the increase will be biggest for exactly the sort of long-form, watched-on-the-television content that a brand most wants to be inside.  That is not a creator inflating a number.  It is the platform doing it, in a direction that happens to flatter both of you, which is the hardest kind of inflation to argue with in a meeting.",
    ],
    "numbers": [("1.10", "YouTube's worked example, a 10 percent uplift"), ("45%", "of YouTube TV impressions co-viewed, per VAB"), ("MAY 2027", "when the figure that lets you check it retires")],
    "flagnote": "This change was announced by YouTube through a Creator Insider Short and two Help Center pages with no named executive attached, and the detailed accounts come from PPC Land and Net Influencer, two small trade outlets, with Dexerto following this morning.  No methodology, error margin, data source, update frequency or accuracy claim has been published for the model, so there is no way to test any individual number it produces.  YouTube's 1.10 co-view rate is a single illustrative example in its own documentation and is not stated to be typical.  The VAB figures come from a trade body that represents premium television and streaming sellers, and a finding that premium streaming is co-viewed more than YouTube is a finding that suits its members; the TVision work behind it carries no sample size here.  The Nielsen comparison is from 2022 and describes a different measurement than the one YouTube has just introduced, so it establishes that co-viewing is common rather than sizing this change.  We could not establish whether historical view counts have been restated or whether only views from 16 November onward carry the uplift, which materially changes how broken a year-on-year comparison is, and nothing we read answers it.  The 15.8 percent television share is Nielsen's August 2026 Gauge and is a share of time, not of views.",
    "so_what": "A view count stopped being a count and became a count plus an estimate, and the estimate is applied most heavily to television viewing, which is the fastest-growing and most brand-friendly part of YouTube.  The honest reading is not that YouTube is cheating.  Counting one television play as one view genuinely did understate how many humans saw something, and the company says as much.  The problem is that a number everyone treats as hard arithmetic now has a model inside it, the model is undocumented, and it arrived less than three months after the last redefinition.  You cannot stop using view counts, but you can stop treating them as the same unit they were in August.",
    "do_this": "Before you present any year-on-year video comparison this week, open Studio Insights, go to Advanced Mode and then Device Type, read the co-view rate for each video, and divide the total views by it so you are comparing plays with plays.  Then switch the headline metric on your own brand channel reporting to engaged views, which exclude co-views, or to watch time, and write the change and the date into the footnote of the deck so the next person who reads it knows which ruler you used.",
}

